{"attack":true,"target_alias":"SRC_001","cited_input_aliases":["SRC_001"],"claim":"Corrective Divergence is logically incompatible with the countercondition of a checklist so unambiguous that disagreement is impossible: a checklist that is unambiguous to all competent readers cannot yield the initial disagreement the target requires, so the target's own marker excludes the very case it must cover.","grounds":"The problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere compliance. The target defines Corrective Divergence: initial internal disagreement on checklist items that converges to the correct answer. In the unambiguous-checklist countercondition, every competent reader reaches the same reading; there is no initial disagreement to record. A single evaluation is statically one run, so the observation token cannot produce divergence that the deterministic checklist precludes.","counterexample":["Checklist item: \"Does the value equal 1? (Yes/No)\"; item value = 1. All panel members read Yes with no divergence.","The target's Corrective Divergence test returns false (no divergence to converge), labeling this mere compliance.","Yet a panel producing exactly the same No->Yes convergence that the target demands would be labeled a genuine reliability gain—identical determinate outputs do not support distinguishing compliance from reliability, and the unambiguous case lacks any initial disagreement to satisfy the divergence criterion."]}